% Values: lists, key order, scalar context, indexing, deep comparison.

### name: val.list.build
--- source
(3, 2, 1)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"3", "2"=t"2", "3"=t"1"}
===
### name: val.list.append-idiom
--- source
A = ("a", "b"); A = (A, "c"); A
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"a", "2"=t"b", "3"=t"c"}
===
### name: val.list.flattens-and-renumbers
--- note
A list operand contributes its children's values, never its keys (§5.9).
--- source
A = ("x", "y"); B = (A, A); B
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"x", "2"=t"y", "3"=t"x", "4"=t"y"}
===
### name: val.list.grouping-passes-one-argument
--- note
F((1,2)) is one list argument; F(1,2) is two.
--- source
COUNT((1, 2))
--- expect
num 2
===
### name: val.scalar-context.first-child
--- source
A = (7, 8); A + 0
--- expect
num 7
===
### name: val.scalar-context.assignment-then-index
--- source
A[1] = 3; A == 3
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.scalar-context.recurses
--- source
A[1][1] = 5; A + 0
--- expect
num 5
===
### name: val.scalar-context.empty-has-none
--- source
FILTER((1, 2), _ > 5) & "x"
--- expect
error E_NO_SCALAR
===
### name: val.index.verbatim-key
--- note
The index is used as a key exactly as written: A[1] and A[1.0] differ.
--- source
A[1] = "a"; HAS(A, "1.0")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.index.missing-key
--- source
A = (1, 2); A[3]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY
===
### name: val.index.on-scalar
--- source
A = 3; A[1]
--- expect
error E_NO_KEY
===
### name: val.undefined-variable
--- source
FOO + 1
--- expect
error E_UNDEF_VAR at 1:1
===
### name: val.reserved-word-as-variable
--- source
IN
--- expect
error E_RESERVED
===
### name: val.assign.autovivifies
--- source
A["x"]["y"] = 1; A
--- expect
tree -{"x"=-{"y"=t"1"}}
===
### name: val.assign.keeps-key-position
--- note
Re-assigning an existing key keeps its original position; order is normative.
--- source
A[1] = "a"; A[2] = "b"; A[1] = "c"; A
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"c", "2"=t"b"}
===
### name: val.count.scalar-has-no-children
--- source
COUNT("abc")
--- expect
num 0
===
### name: val.indexes
--- source
A["x"] = 1; A["y"] = 2; INDEXES(A)
--- expect
tree -{"1"=t"x", "2"=t"y"}
===
### name: val.has
--- source
A["x"] = 1; HAS(A, "x") XOR HAS(A, "z")
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.eql.structural
--- source
(1, 2) EQL (1, 2)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.eql.is-not-numeric
--- note
EQL compares structurally, so scale is visible where == would ignore it.
--- source
"5.00" EQL "5"
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.eql.key-order-matters
--- source
A["x"] = 1; A["y"] = 2; B["y"] = 2; B["x"] = 1; A EQL B
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.eql.kind-matters
--- source
"A" EQL FROM_HEX("41")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.in.list
--- source
2 IN (1, 2, 3)
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
### name: val.in.absent
--- source
"PL" IN ("DE", "CZ")
--- expect
bool FALSE
===
### name: val.in.scalar-compares-directly
--- source
"x" IN "x"
--- expect
bool TRUE
===
